WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.937
Here is the workflow I want you to use whenever you build a join.

00:00:03.937 --> 00:00:12.487
First, define the grain. Before writing SQL, say out loud what one row in the final result should represent.

00:00:12.487 --> 00:00:17.377
Maybe it is one patient, one admission, one student registration, or one sales transaction.

00:00:17.377 --> 00:00:22.632
If you cannot describe the target grain clearly, the query can become confusing very quickly.

00:00:22.632 --> 00:00:25.737
Second, identify the row sets you are combining.

00:00:25.737 --> 00:00:29.302
Third, decide the matching rule.

00:00:29.302 --> 00:00:32.782
Usually the relationship is based on a key such as patient_id, customer_id, or course_id.

00:00:32.782 --> 00:00:40.157
But do not assume that two columns with the same name automatically represent the correct relationship.

00:00:40.157 --> 00:00:43.522
Fourth, decide which unmatched rows must stay.

00:00:43.522 --> 00:00:50.837
Suppose a public health analyst wants a list of every patient, including people with no lab results.

00:00:50.837 --> 00:00:54.647
That requirement tells you that the patient table must be preserved.

00:00:54.647 --> 00:01:01.407
If the question is only about patients who have lab results, then unmatched patients do not need to remain.

00:01:01.407 --> 00:01:04.952
Fifth, predict cardinality. Can one patient have many admissions?

00:01:04.952 --> 00:01:08.077
Can one admission have many lab tests?

00:01:08.077 --> 00:01:13.572
Finally, validate the output. Compare what you expected with what SQL actually returned.

00:01:13.572 --> 00:01:20.240
This workflow may feel slower at first, but it prevents many of the errors that are hardest to notice later.
